=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139534496 17592186045417 false] [17592186119497 1074 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n![](images/53/53707f60f3888da139fde8caa9c8310c1cb1c5884f3ffe39669d768f26a87a71.svg)\nTo see why, notice that \n![](images/92/9235ccd66098682ac98b70ab41c270a7a88d2d9ef8baa6eb27a395f7771374c1.svg)\nIn other words, the vectors $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are linearly dependent, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{a}_1  +x_2  \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=\\dfrac 1 3$ and $x_2=-1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186119498 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](images/a1/a1275962afa7cdb43c691eaeb65656491c546b6272571a91924f4e8ac15fd96d.svg)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nII. $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186119498 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](images/e9/e9d8c1ab6f31d1ca580343f16d10eb55b6aae24db679129540a30b552cedaf19.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0.$\n\n- The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534496 17592186045417 false] [17592186119498 1074 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186119498 1074 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534496 17592186045417 false] [17592186119499 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](images/8d/8d88cae0e40e7a377314e359086dc45fa0460c17ed55fe379cc15537886b2623.png)\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2,$ and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." 13194139534496 17592186045417 false] [17592186119499 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](images/ae/ae2f35f46132153c89cbfe418d1a316586e623e0ff9819ac50535fc3e3fd95db.svg)\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2,$ and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\nII. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186119499 1074 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2,$ while $x_3$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true." 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186119499 1074 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2,$ while $x_3$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true." 13194139534496 17592186045417 false] [17592186119923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423131 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186120052 1050 600.0 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186120147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186120147 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186120147 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $2a_{1} - a_{2} + a_{3} = 0$.\n2. $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = 0$ only if $x_{1} = x_{2} = x_{3} = 0$.\n3. The set ${a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534496 17592186045417 false] [17592186120147 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+{a}_{3} & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-3+1 \\\\ -2-0+2 \\\\ 4-0+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186120148 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534496 17592186045417 false] [17592186120148 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186120148 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186120148 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534496 17592186045417 false] [17592186121122 1064 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/46/46c22d599752ded730a67bfd57b4dfe238e512f6dff2d561afaf2f145c8118c2.svg)\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n![](images/14/145f500e491087587478f03bd52ae180a7b4237acf17db0317ca07d28f389c56.svg)\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." 13194139534496 17592186045417 true] [17592186121122 1064 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." 13194139534496 17592186045417 false] [17592186121124 1064 "So far, we've discussed linear dependence and linear independence for sets of two vectors. However, these concepts can be extended to sets of any number of vectors.\n\nA set of $n$ vectors $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is called **linearly in